course: dictionary
lesson: 11963
title: Catalyst and change
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A catalyst is a word with two meanings, and both are about something that starts or speeds up a change. «The enzymes in your mouth are a catalyst that helps you digest food faster.» The clue is faster: a catalyst makes something happen more quickly.
explain: In science, read the words after the word. «The catalyst made the reaction go faster, and it was still there at the end.» The clue is still there at the end, so the catalyst is not used up. In history or stories, a catalyst is a person or event: «The new bridge became a catalyst for change, and soon there were shops and houses nearby.»
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If you put in something that started the change, such as «The new bridge became something that started the change for change», the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right idea.
fact: Catalyst is a noun: a catalyst, catalysts.
fact: «Catalyst» means a substance that makes a chemical reaction go faster without being used up itself.
fact: «Catalyst» can also mean a person or event that makes a big change happen, or makes it happen sooner.
fact: «Acted as a catalyst» means helped to start or speed up a change.
fact: Enzymes in your body and the catalytic converter in a car are both examples of catalysts in science.
exercise: «The new bridge became a catalyst for change.» What does catalyst mean here? | A bridge made of metal | A type of old boat | A chemical that is used up | *A person or event that makes a big change happen
exercise: In science, a catalyst makes which thing go faster? | A rainstorm | A car journey | *A chemical reaction | A football match
exercise: Which of these is a catalyst in the science sense? | *An enzyme in your body that helps digest food | A bag of crisps that is eaten | A cup that holds water | A ticket that is used up at the station
test: «The argument was the catalyst for the war.» Catalyst here means: | A peace treaty | *The event that set off the war | A holiday | A weapon
test: A catalyst is not used up in a reaction, so afterwards it is: | Gone | *Still there, unchanged | Turned into water | Bigger
exam: «Her speech acted as a catalyst, and soon the whole town was helping.» What does acted as a catalyst mean? | Ended a long argument | *Started or speeded up a change | Did nothing at all | Made the town quieter
